    "slug": "un-warns-sudan-conflict-threatens-education-of-8-million-children",
    "summary": "The United Nations has warned that Sudan's ongoing civil war endangers the future of an entire generation, with over 8 million school-age children out of school. UN Deputy Secretary-General Amina Mohamed issued the alert during a Security Council meeting on protecting children's education in Sudan.",
    "content": "The United Nations has issued a stark warning about the impact of Sudan's civil war on the country's youth, stating that the futures of millions of children are at risk. In a statement delivered on Friday, UN Deputy Secretary-General Amina Mohamed emphasized that more than 8 million children of school age are currently not attending classes due to the conflict. The warning came during an informal meeting of the UN Security Council dedicated to safeguarding education for Sudanese children.\n\nThe conflict between the Sudanese Armed Forces (SAF) and the Rapid Support Forces (RSF) has entered its third year, with no end in sight. The fighting has severely disrupted daily life across the country, with schools either damaged, occupied by armed groups, or functioning under unsafe conditions. Many families have been displaced multiple times, making regular attendance at educational institutions nearly impossible.\n\nThe UN's concern is not limited to immediate academic setbacks. The broader implications for a generation growing up amid violence and instability are profound. Education provides not only knowledge but also a structure for social development and future economic participation. Without it, the risk of long-term cycles of poverty and conflict increases substantially.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this crisis resonates deeply. Many Somalis have personal connections to Sudan, whether through family ties, economic links, or shared historical experiences in the Horn of Africa. The displacement of millions of Sudanese children mirrors similar challenges seen in Somalia's own recent history, making this issue particularly relevant. Diaspora communities are often among the first to respond to humanitarian needs, and this situation calls for their continued attention and support.\n\nThe international community has been called upon to prioritize the protection of education in Sudan. The UN has urged both parties to the conflict to respect international law and ensure that schools remain safe havens. Without immediate and sustained action, the warning suggests that a whole generation of Sudanese children could be lost to the chaos of war.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/08/08/dagaalka-suudaan-oo-halis-geliyay-mustaqbalka-jiil-dhan-iyo-qaramada-midoobay-oo-digniin-culus-soo-saartay/",
